.TH PYMEDIA 1 "November 2026" "pyMedia" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
pymedia \- command-line media processing tool based on FFmpeg
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pymedia
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pyMedia
is a command-line tool for processing audio and video media using
.BR ffmpeg (1)
and
.BR ffprobe (1).

It provides higher-level commands for common media-processing operations:
metadata inspection, thumbnail sheets, container joining, splitting and
remuxing, profile-based transcoding, audio and subtitles track editing and
animated image generation. It also handles input validation, output-path
resolution and output-name conflicts, and shows a progress bar for long
operations.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Analysis
.TP
.B info
Show the metadata of a media file (codecs, streams, size, duration).

.TP
.B sheet
Generate a thumbnail-grid sheet with a media-info header. Select the layout
with
.B --preset
.I fhd
/
.I hd
/
.I web
(default:
.BR hd ).
.SS Video
.TP
.B join
Concatenate two or more video files into a single media container. The output
file is required.
.TP
.B remux
Change the container and metadata without transcoding.
.B --fast-start
moves the index to the beginning of the file (MP4 output only),
.B --genpts
regenerates broken timestamps and
.B --sort-tracks
orders the stream tracks by type and language.
.TP
.B cut
Cut a section of a media file with
.B --start
and
.B --end
, or split it into segments at the timestamps given with
.B --at
(comma-separated,
.I hh:mm:ss
). Segments are written as
.I name_000.ext
,
.I name_001.ext
and so on.
.TP
.B transcode
Transcode the video track and/or selected audio tracks
.RB ( --video ,
.B --audio
.I int,int,...
) using a profile selected with
.B --preset
.I fast
/
.I even
/
.I slow
and applying the requested filters. At least one transcoding option or filter
is required.
.SS Audio
.TP
.B add-audio
Add an audio track to a media file. The language is set with
.B --language
.I code
(ISO 639-2).
.TP
.B delete-audio
Delete audio tracks. Without
.B --tracks
(comma-separated indices) all audio tracks are removed.
.TP
.B edit-audio
Edit the metadata of an audio track selected with
.B --track
.I int
using
.BR --language ,
.BR --title ,
.BR --default ,
.BR --forced ,
.BR --commentary
or
.BR --hearing-impaired .
.TP
.B extract-audio
Extract audio tracks to standalone audio files. Without
.B --tracks
all audio tracks are extracted.
.SS Subtitles
.TP
.B add-subs
Add a subtitles track (SRT, ASS, SSA) to a media file. The language is set
with
.B --language
.I code
(ISO 639-2).
.TP
.B delete-subs
Delete subtitles tracks. Without
.B --tracks
(comma-separated indices) all subtitles are removed.
.TP
.B edit-subs
Edit the metadata of a subtitles track selected with
.B --track
.I int
using
.BR --language ,
.BR --title ,
.BR --default ,
.BR --forced ,
.BR --hearing-impaired
or
.BR --visual-impaired .
.TP
.B extract-subs
Extract subtitles tracks to standalone files. Without
.B --tracks
all subtitles are extracted.
.SS Image
.TP
.B animated
Convert a video clip into an animated image (GIF by default). The time range
and frame rate are set with
.BR --start ,
.BR --end
and
.B --fps
(4\-20).
.TP
.B frames
Capture thumbnails at specific timestamps given with
.B --at
(comma-separated,
.I hh:mm:ss
).
.TP
.B interval
Capture thumbnails at regular intervals given with
.B --every
.I seconds
, optionally bounded by
.BR --start
and
.BR --end .
.TP
.B scene
Capture thumbnails at scene changes using a sensitivity threshold set with
.B --scene
(0.1\-0.5).
.SH OPTIONS
The common options are accepted by every command:
.TP
.B --help
Display the localized help of the application or of the selected command and
exit.
.TP
.BR -o ", " --output
.I path
Output file path.
.TP
.BR -d ", " --directory
.I dir
Output directory for batch processing. Mutually exclusive with
.BR --output .
.TP
.B --overwrite
.I mode
Policy when the output file already exists:
.BR yes ,
.BR no
or
.BR ask
(default:
.BR ask ).
.TP
.B --debug
Enable DEBUG log level.

Image and video commands also accept the following filters:
.TP
.B --crop
.I WIDTH,HEIGHT,X,Y
Crop to WIDTH x HEIGHT at the offset (X, Y) from the top-left corner.
.TP
.B --rotate
.I 90
/
.I 180
/
.I 270
Rotate the image by an orthogonal angle.
.TP
.B --size
.I WIDTHxHEIGHT
Target resolution in pixels.
.TP
.B --mode
.I fit
/
.I stretch
/
.I cover
Scaling policy (default:
.BR fit ).
.TP
.B --upscale
Allow upscaling beyond the source dimensions.
.TP
.BR --hflip ", " --vflip
Flip the image horizontally or vertically.
.SH OUTPUT CONFLICTS
When an output file already exists, pyMedia resolves the conflict according
to the selected overwrite mode:
.TP
.B yes
Replace the existing output file.
.TP
.B no
Skip the process when a destination already exists.
.TP
.B ask
Prompt for confirmation before replacing an existing file. The thumbnail
commands
.RB ( frames ,
.BR interval ,
.BR scene )
default to
.BR yes ;
the rest of the commands default to
.BR ask .
.SH INPUTS
Commands operate on media files supported by the underlying FFmpeg
installation. pyMedia uses
.BR ffprobe (1)
to inspect media and obtain the information required by processing
operations.
.SH OUTPUTS
Output files are generated by FFmpeg. When no output path is provided, a
default name is derived from the input file using the default container of
the media type (see CONFIGURATION). Output paths may be affected by the
configured overwrite mode when a destination already exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pymedia info input.mp4
Show the metadata of a media file.
.TP
.B pymedia remux input.mp4 -o output.mkv
Change the container without transcoding.
.TP
.B pymedia join part1.mp4 part2.mp4 -o movie.mp4
Concatenate two media files.
.TP
.B pymedia cut input.mp4 --at 10:05,40:30
Split a media file into three segments.
.TP
.B pymedia transcode source.mp4 --preset slow --video --audio 1
Transcode the video track and audio track 1 using the "slow" profile.
.TP
.B pymedia add-audio input.mp4 audio.m4a --language eng
Add an English audio track.
.TP
.B pymedia add-subs input.mp4 subs_es.srt --language spa --default
Add Spanish subtitles as the default track.
.TP
.B pymedia animated input.mp4 --start 00:00:05 --end 00:00:12
Create an animated image from a time range.
.TP
.B pymedia frames input.mp4 --at 00:01:30,00:05:15
Capture thumbnails at two timestamps.
.TP
.B pymedia interval input.mp4 --every 5
Capture a thumbnail every five seconds.
.SH CONFIGURATION
pyMedia stores persistent application configuration in the user's
configuration directory. On Linux the default location is:

.nf
~/.config/pymedia/config.toml
.fi

The file is created on first run from the bundled defaults and is read on
each invocation. Main settings:

.TP
.B [app] language
Interface language:
.BR system ,
.BR spanish
or
.BR english .
.TP
.B [app] stall_timeout
Seconds (30\-600) after which a stalled ffmpeg process is killed. Default: 60.
.TP
.B [default_containers]
Default output extensions per media type: animated image (.gif), audio track
(.m4a), image (.jpg), media (.mp4) and subtitles (.srt).
.TP
.B [transcode]
Transcode profiles (
.BR fast ,
.BR even ,
.BR slow ) defining the video and audio codecs, the video CRF and preset, and
the audio bit rate.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pymedia/config.toml
User configuration, created on first run from the bundled defaults.
.TP
.I ~/.config/pymedia/logging.log
Application log with timestamps, levels and messages.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PYMEDIA_LOCALEDIR
Optional directory containing the gettext catalogs
.RB ( \fIlang\fP /LC_MESSAGES/pymedia.mo ),
overrideable for frozen builds.

pyMedia relies on
.BR ffmpeg (1)
and
.BR ffprobe (1)
being available to the application. The exact executable resolution mechanism
depends on the installation and runtime environment.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.BR ffmpeg (1)
Media processing backend.
.TP
.BR ffprobe (1)
Media inspection and metadata extraction backend.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The operation completed successfully.
.TP
.B non-zero
The operation failed. Errors may result from invalid input, incompatible
media, an existing output file according to the overwrite mode, an FFmpeg
execution failure (including stall timeouts) or another processing error.
.SH NOTES
pyMedia is a higher-level interface to FFmpeg. It does not replace FFmpeg's
full command-line interface. For operations requiring functionality not
exposed by pyMedia, use FFmpeg directly.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1)
